186188613 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186185958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188613 #edb/ref 1069 "q-145085" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188613 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188613 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the polar curve $r = θ^{2}\\cos θ$ for $θ ∈ (- π, π)$. Given that\n\n$x^{′}(θ) = 2θ\\cos^{2} (θ) - θ^{2}\\sin (2θ), y^{′}(θ) = θ\\sin (2θ) + θ^{2}\\cos (2θ)$,\nand $x^{′}(0) = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y^{′}(0) = 0$\n2. $lim_(θ → 0^{-})\\frac{dy}{dx} = ∞$\n3. The curve has a vertical tangent at $θ = 0$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188613 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8c055904-2b51-5183-9e8c-f8a23f95c6d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188613 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189087 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189087 #edb/ref 1061 37500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189087 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c57d45c4-8a9c-599f-993b-2326fc1fb56f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189087 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186189367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189103 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189103 #edb/ref 1061 37995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189103 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f33de88b-4d60-5405-829e-a88dd1826520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189103 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186189374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189116 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189116 #edb/ref 1061 38153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189116 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ecb3fe0f-d538-57b4-b4e4-a660ce2892d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189116 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186189380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189117 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189117 #edb/ref 1061 38154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189117 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5c1c429e-ced3-5909-92a2-519ae11a4322" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189117 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189146 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189146 #edb/ref 1061 38606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189146 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8f383aba-74ba-5583-9105-a9ee0b48e2fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189146 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186189391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189147 #edb/ref 1061 38614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189147 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9390067a-786b-5a47-889a-88feebe31790" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189147 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189367 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the curve $r = 1-\\sin\\theta$ for $0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/09/09b25bca42f62b6567cecf4b14987113003ccea6dd869a569be75af4fa1781c9.png)\n\nUsing previously discussed techniques, it's straightforward to show that at point $C$ where $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)} = \\dfrac00.\n$$\n\nTo see what's happening, let's zoom in and observe the behavior of the curve at this point. \n\n![](images/00/001914b359c9c95f6a8fceb655a5233242065eb4231765bcfc0d652ebbf4eed8.png)\n\nIf we look carefully, we notice that the tangent lines become steeper and steeper as we approach the point $C$ from either direction.\n\n![](images/40/40de3bbd7f8422ff4ab4af6091497579f8031b8a43296e466f8c23ed4a121097.png)\n\nTherefore, as we take the *limit* as we approach $C,$ it would appear that these tangent lines become vertical. \n\n![](images/ca/caf6255e2ae97865da52dec64413e2e61a9a807f50b54ae4eae2f2b96f030c30.png)\n\nWhen the limit of the tangent lines becomes vertical like this, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at $C.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189367 #edb/ref 1065 6185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189367 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "69760698-2d63-55ca-aa44-895e4a5a7c66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189367 #edb/ref 1067 "Vertical Tangents to Polar Curves in Non-Differentiable Cases" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189374 #edb/ref 1064 "So, it seems that the curve $r = 1-\\sin\\theta$ has a vertical tangent at $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2.$ However, to be sure of this, we need to check that the tangent lines' slopes grow to infinity as we approach $\\theta = \\dfrac \\pi 2.$ In other words, we need to check that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^{-}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^{+}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet's now verify that this is true:\n\nUsing techniques discussed in previous lessons, it's straightforward to show that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta-\\cos(2\\theta), \\qquad \ny'(\\theta)=\\cos\\theta - \\sin2\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that substituting $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2$ gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00,$ so evaluating this limit by direct substitution won't work. However, we can evaluate this limit using L'Hopital's rule.\n\n- Applying L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the left-sided limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the limit in the denominator, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^-} \\left(\\cos\\theta - 2\\sin(2\\theta)\\right) = 0^-,\n$$\n\nwhere the notation $0^-$ means that the denominator approaches zero from the negative numbers.  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Using similar methods, it can be shown that the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, we conclude that there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ \n\n![](images/ea/ea0a03e575d962bd8d69505e20166dcc60c77cfe933f30762bb81296ccf7a797.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189374 #edb/ref 1065 6269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189374 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7b372073-8035-5855-9367-f29c35485805" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189374 #edb/ref 1067 "Using Limits to Find Vertical Tangents" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189380 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the curve $r = 1+\\cos\\theta$ for $0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e72d6a7d4b490a37f99723d0cde12a378f038b43ba373547e9c8feaf71952717.png)\n\nUsing previously discussed techniques, it's straightforward to show that at point $C$ where $\\theta = \\pi,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)} = \\dfrac00.\n$$\n\nTo see what's happening at this point, let's zoom in and observe the behavior of the curve.\n\n![](images/d0/d03ca27e8cecd32aebc0e6914ac2ab128bce57ab876b96e8aaa91b562041ba4c.png)\n\nIf we look carefully, we notice that the tangent lines flatten out as we approach the point $C$ from either direction.\n\n![](images/4f/4fda95ed912c83ac36ec772b6afa6554ad8a81b7c98a330fd774d501b4bbecdf.png)\n\nTherefore, as we take the *limit* of the tangent lines as we approach $C,$ it would appear that they become horizontal. \n\n![](images/50/50f8bd688c55d8458a9db100469cd62138efc200402496d3adb18f90ea53e747.png)\n\nWhen the limit of the tangent lines becomes horizontal like this, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at $C.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189380 #edb/ref 1065 6303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189380 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e9679f34-9ef1-55a0-ba1a-15cccaecb4a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189380 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189391 #edb/ref 1064 "So, it seems that the curve $r = 1+\\cos\\theta$ has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta = \\pi.$ However, to be sure of this, we need to check that the tangent lines flatten out as we approach $\\theta = \\pi.$ In other words, we need to check that\n\n \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\pi} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0.\n$$\n\nLet's now verify that this is true:\n\nUsing techniques discussed in previous lessons, it's straightforward to show that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta(2\\cos\\theta+1), \\qquad \ny'(\\theta)=2\\cos^2\\theta +\\cos\\theta - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that substituting $\\theta = \\pi$ gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00,$ so evaluating this limit by direct substitution won't work. However, we can evaluate this limit using L'Hopital's rule.\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{(2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (0)+0}{2\\cdot 1+(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\pi} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0\n$$\n\nwe conclude that our curve does indeed have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta = \\pi.$\n\n![](images/f1/f172dbe1e7fa97fa924feed4ee193a620b1224a985a4e406ef4e46afbdc0cb2a.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189391 #edb/ref 1065 6384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189391 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6a54c219-b6df-5e73-8673-14f48b1d69af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189391 #edb/ref 1067 "Using Limits to Find Horizontal Tangents" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569437 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186570054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569437 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186570055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569437 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186570057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569437 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186570062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569437 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186570065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569437 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569437 #edb/ref 1045 "Horizontal and Vertical Tangents to Polar Curves in Non-Differentiable Cases" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569437 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186570055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569437 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569437 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569437 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "3c665c19-95e5-52b1-a4a9-44e5ed397b25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569468 #edb